it's expensive and impractical and it  doesn't always guarantee results but  tracking terrorist suspects is one of  the most important roles for mi5 and our  police so how do they do it  mi5 have around 3,000 subjects of  interest or SOS but they can't keep an  eye on all of them all of the time  24-hour surveillance takes at least two  teams of 15 specialists each working a  12-hour shift long-term observation may  take up to four teams which could mean  around 60 people add in undercover cars  vans and motorbikes as well as all the  surveillance gear and this becomes an  expensive job and remember this is all  for one person 60 people for all 3,000  suspects adds up to 180 thousand  officers and spooks and that's far more  staff than all of the UK police and mi5  have combined so they have to pick their  targets carefully when mi5 received new  intelligence that kicks off a formal  process they check the suspect for links  to any existing investigations check  across the mi5 database to see if they  have any information on them and then  try to assess just how much of a threat  they pose the investigations are sorted  into four priority categories  re-engagement risk uncorroborated  intelligence extremist activity and the  highest priority attack planning the SOS  are also put into tiers tier one main  targets tier two key contacts of the  main targets and tier three contacts of  the tier one and two targets once they  decide which investigations are the most  urgent the surveillance begins how  effective is this process well we really  don't know the government say they  stopped at least 22 attacks in the past  few years but some of the highest  profile terror attacks in the UK in  recent years were carried out by SOS the  suspect had been recently released from  prison where he had been serving a  sentence for Islamist related terrorism  offences Westminster Bridge attacker  Khalid Masood and Manchester Arena  bomber Salman Abady were both considered  closed subjects of interest Stratham  attacker Sudesh a man had also recently  been released from prison as had the  2019 London Bridge attacker Osman Khan  whether these people should have been  considered a higher priority is up for  debate but it goes to show just how  difficult it really is to track terror  suspects and how high the price can be  if they slip through the hands of the  authorities
 

